SELECT 'stool-land-concurrence-ghana-explained', 'Stool Land Concurrence in Ghana: The Hidden Consent That Blocks Transfers', '<p class="tldr"><strong>TL;DR:</strong> On stool, skin or family land (≈80% of Ghana), transfers need the custodian''s documented concurrence <em>plus</em> Lands Commission registration. A seller''s indenture without concurrence is an incomplete transfer that the Commission can reject later.</p><h2>What is concurrence and why does it exist?</h2><p>Ghana runs a dual tenure system: chiefs and family heads hold allodial title as custodians; occupants hold lesser interests. <strong>Concurrence</strong> is the custodian''s formal consent to your specific grant - proof the seller had something to sell. Skip it and you bought a lawsuit, not land.</p><h2>When is concurrence required?</h2><p>Whenever the root title is stool, skin, clan or family land - i.e., most land outside state grants and old registered estates. Your lawyer confirms root title at the search stage; if customary, concurrence documents must name your transaction specifically, not some decade-old general consent.</p><h2>How do you secure it properly?</h2><p>1. Identify the <strong>correct custodian</strong> (paramount vs divisional vs family head - get this wrong and concurrence is void). 2. Obtain written concurrence referencing plot, parties, date. 3. File it with the indenture at the Lands Commission. 4. Budget ground-rent obligations to the stool going forward - concealed arrears are a classic hidden cost.</p><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul><li>~80% of Ghana''s land needs a custodian''s consent trail.</li><li>Concurrence must match your transaction, not a generic old letter.</li><li>Unpaid stool ground rent transfers with the land - check arrears.</li></ul><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><p><strong>Can a chief sell stool land outright?</strong><br>Chiefs grant interests as custodians; outright ''sales'' bypassing custom and registration are exactly what courts unwind.</p><p><strong>What if two chiefs claim the same land?</strong><br>Walk away. Chieftaincy disputes take years; your money waits in limbo while they litigate.</p><p><strong>Does concurrence replace registration?</strong><br>No - you need both. Concurrence satisfies custom; registration satisfies statute. Either alone is insufficient.</p><p><em>Sources: Diaspora Affairs GH land reform brief; Land Act 2020 (Act 1036).</em></p><p><em>Buying land in Ghana? <a href=''/workspace.html''>Sporafind</a> maps verified diaspora lawyers, surveyors and REAC-registered agents - diligence before deposits.</em></p>', 'Your seller has documents - but the chief never concurred. What stool/skin concurrence is, when you need it, and how missing consent kills transfers.', ARRAY['land','stool land','customary','concurrence','title'], 'Land & Title', 'published', '2026-10-09T08:00:00.000Z', 'Stool Land Concurrence in Ghana Explained', 'Seller has papers but the chief never concurred? Stool land concurrence explained: when you need it and how missing consent blocks your transfer.'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM blog_posts WHERE slug = 'stool-land-concurrence-ghana-explained');
