SELECT 'ramsar-wetland-land-ghana-demolition-risk', 'Ramsar, Wetlands & Waterways: The Ghana Plots That Get Demolished', '<p class="tldr"><strong>TL;DR:</strong> Never buy without checking the location class: Ramsar sites, wetlands, waterways, dam downstream zones and road/government reservations get built on, flooded - then demolished. Verify with survey plan + Lands Commission + assembly planning before paying anything.</p><h2>Which locations are automatic no-go zones?</h2><p><strong>Ramsar sites and wetlands</strong> (protected), <strong>waterways and floodplains</strong>, <strong>downstream of dams</strong> (gates open to save the structure - Weija-area residents flood yearly), <strong>road/airport/utility reservations</strong>, and <strong>mineral-rich land</strong> where concessions override you. Sellers of these plots compete on price because the land''s legal future is zero.</p><h2>How do you check a specific location?</h2><p>1. Licensed surveyor''s site plan overlaid on planning schemes. 2. Lands Commission search for zoning/encumbrance flags. 3. Municipal assembly planning office confirmation. 4. Physical visit in <strong>rainy season</strong> - dry-season plots lie. 5. Neighbour interviews: ''does it flood here?'' beats any brochure.</p><h2>Why does location also price everything else?</h2><p>Flood risk, road quality and litigation history directly set land value - a ''cheap'' plot in a dispute/flood zone is the most expensive land you''ll ever buy once demolition, litigation or fill-costs land.</p><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul><li>Water-adjacent discounts are demolition-risk discounts.</li><li>Visit in the rains; interview neighbours; check the assembly.</li><li>Reservations and Ramsar designations are non-negotiable.</li></ul><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><p><strong>Can I get compensation if demolished?</strong><br>Effectively no, if you built on a protected/reserved zone - courts treat it as illegal development.</p><p><strong>Are hillside plots safer?</strong><br>From floods, yes; from erosion and road washouts, no. Every terrain has its tax - price it.</p><p><strong>Do developers handle this?</strong><br>Licensed developers do - another reason registered developments beat raw plots for remote buyers.</p><p><em>Sources: Africa Building Hub location series; Ghana EPA Ramsar designations.</em></p><p><em>Buying land in Ghana? <a href=''/workspace.html''>Sporafind</a> maps verified diaspora lawyers, surveyors and REAC-registered agents - diligence before deposits.</em></p>', 'Cheap land near water in Accra ends in bulldozers. Ramsar sites, wetlands, dams and road reservations - how to check location before you buy.', ARRAY['land','flooding','Ramsar','location','Accra'], 'Land & Title', 'published', '2026-10-09T08:00:00.000Z', 'Ramsar & Wetland Plots in Ghana: Demolition Risk', 'Cheap plots near water end in bulldozers. Ramsar sites, wetlands and reservations explained - plus how to check any location before paying.'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM blog_posts WHERE slug = 'ramsar-wetland-land-ghana-demolition-risk');
