SELECT 'malaria-vaccinations-ghana-returnee-health-brief', 'Malaria & Vaccinations for Ghana: The Returnee Health Brief', '<p class="tldr"><strong>TL;DR:</strong> Take malaria seriously from day one: prophylaxis per medical advice, yellow fever certificate for entry, routine boosters current, plus nightly net + repellent + screened-sleep discipline. Prevention costs little; treatment costs trips.</p><h2>What shots do you need?</h2><p><strong>Yellow fever:</strong> certificate often required at entry - carry it. <strong>Routine:</strong> MMR, DTaP/Tdap, polio current. <strong>Consider:</strong> typhoid, hepatitis A/B, meningitis (belt season), rabies (animal exposure risk). Confirm with a travel clinic 6-8 weeks pre-departure - some series need spacing.</p><h2>What is the malaria strategy?</h2><p>Prophylaxis choice is personal-medical (resistance, tolerance, duration) - decide with your doctor, not forums. Layer: treated nets, evening repellent, screened bedrooms, drained compounds, AC (mosquitoes dislike cool sealed rooms). Test fevers early - rapid tests are widely available; severe malaria punishes delay.</p><h2>How do kids change the calculus?</h2><p>Paediatric dosing, school-trip prophylaxis continuity, and net discipline as non-negotiable routine. Brief schools on your child''s plan and keep emergency antimalarials only as doctor-directed backup.</p><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul><li>Certificate, prophylaxis, boosters - before the flight.</li><li>Nets + repellent + screens: every night, no exceptions.</li><li>Fever means test-first, fast.</li></ul><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><p><strong>Do long-stayers still need prophylaxis?</strong><br>Medical advice varies by profile - many transition to bite-avoidance + early treatment; decide with your doctor.</p><p><strong>Are vaccines available locally?</strong><br>Yes at private facilities and public campaigns - but arrive covered, then boost locally.</p><p><strong>What about water-borne illness?</strong><br>Treated/filtered drinking water only; street-food wisdom (hot, fresh, busy stalls) applies.</p><p><em>Sources: GDAAR; travel-medicine guidance; returnee practice.</em></p><p><em>Moving with family? <a href=''/workspace.html''>Sporafind</a> finds diaspora parents at your shortlisted schools and doctors returnees actually use - real experience before you commit.</em></p>', 'Prophylaxis strategy, yellow fever proof, routine boosters, and the net-repellent-screen discipline that keeps families safe.', ARRAY['malaria','vaccinations','health','prevention'], 'Relocation Guides', 'published', '2026-10-25T08:00:00.000Z', 'Malaria & Vaccinations: Returnee Brief', 'Prophylaxis, yellow fever proof, boosters, and bite discipline: the preventive health brief for Ghana returnees.'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM blog_posts WHERE slug = 'malaria-vaccinations-ghana-returnee-health-brief');
